WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:06.600
Dell’s long-awaited XPS 13 2-in-1 is finally
right here in our office, packing Intel’s

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:10.180
10th-gen Ice Lake 10nm CPU, which is a huge
deal!

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:12.981
And I’ve gotta say that I couldn’t be
more impressed by it.

00:00:12.981 --> 00:00:18.060
It seems like Dell just went all out on this
laptop this year, fixing so many of the issues

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.010
we had with the previous XPS 13.

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:25.490
In this review, we’re gonna look at basically
everything from build quality, performance,

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:28.189
speakers, display quality, and much more!

00:00:28.189 --> 00:00:33.470
But first, A huge Thanks to Intel and Micro
Center for Providing us with this Dell XPS.

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:38.460
Micro Center has 25 stores nationwide with
an impressive variety of electronics.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:44.250
From Gaming, VR, computer parts like processors,
graphics, and everything else needed to build

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:45.250
a PC.

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:49.469
They also have a huge variety of desktops,
laptops, and all the accessories that you

00:00:49.469 --> 00:00:50.469
need.

00:00:50.469 --> 00:00:53.739
Not only that ebut also highly trained staff
and low prices.

00:00:53.739 --> 00:00:57.940
Check out a local Micro Center today to get
your hands-on a variety of products before

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:05.880
purchasing or use the link below to view the
2019 Dell XPS 2 in 1 at microcenter.com.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:09.691
It seems like this year, Dell made it their
mission to fix the various things that we

00:01:09.691 --> 00:01:14.079
had issues with in the past, I’ll explain
all of those changes in a minute, but first,

00:01:14.079 --> 00:01:18.460
I want to talk about why the new 10nm Ice
Lake processor is such a big deal!

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:22.799
Intel usually upgrades their architecture
every 2 years, but it’s been 4 years since

00:01:22.799 --> 00:01:27.130
the last upgrade, so they’ve been working
on 10nm tech for a long time!

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:33.920
And they definitely got it right, with WiFi
6 support, incredibly fast LPDDR4X RAM support,

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:36.890
and upgraded Iris Plus graphics in more of
their chips.

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:40.549
The main benefit is that it’s much more
efficient, so you’re getting more performance

00:01:40.549 --> 00:01:45.880
while using less power and staying cooler
than Dell’s recent standard XPS 13 laptop.

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:49.849
So you should see better battery life than
on that model, it should feel cooler, and

00:01:49.849 --> 00:01:54.159
the dual fans shouldn’t be as loud because
of the new vapor chambers and hidden exhaust

00:01:54.159 --> 00:01:57.749
venting near the hinge that won’t ever get
blocked by your lap.

00:01:57.749 --> 00:02:03.189
Compared to the 2017 model, you’ll get about
2 hours better battery life on the Full HD

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:04.189
model.

00:02:04.189 --> 00:02:08.110
However, this model has the 4K display, so
it definitely won’t get as much battery

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:09.509
life as the Full HD model.

00:02:09.509 --> 00:02:16.940
It’s also packing the 4-core 1.5GHz 10nm
processor, as well as a massive 32GB of 3733MHz

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:22.270
RAM and a 1TB NVMe SSD.

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:26.090
Before we get into the really impressive performance
benchmarks, let’s talk about the things

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:29.030
Dell has improved with this new XPS 13 2-in-1.

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:33.939
First off, it’s super compact, with it being
impressively thin, light and smaller than

00:02:33.939 --> 00:02:39.019
you’d expect from a 13” laptop, especially
one with a hinge that can fold all the back.

00:02:39.019 --> 00:02:43.801
And when you do use it like that in tablet
mode with its touchscreen display, and there

00:02:43.801 --> 00:02:46.920
seems to be magnets that hold both sides together
nicely.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:51.400
You can even configure Windows to automatically
switch to tablet mode, and then use the optional

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:52.400
pen that can magnetically attach to the side
of the XPS.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:56.030
Build quality is better than its ever been
since it’s machined out of a solid piece

00:02:56.030 --> 00:03:00.400
of aluminum, just like MacBooks have been
for years, which are known for their exceptional

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:01.430
build quality.

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:05.000
You can tell that it’s solid aluminum by
looking at the sides of the laptop around

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:08.489
the ports, whereas it used to be plastic with
thin aluminum covers.

00:03:08.489 --> 00:03:12.299
On the bottom, Dell has added extra vents
on the sides, with two of them being used

00:03:12.299 --> 00:03:16.349
for the speakers, and they’ve also redesigned
some of the air vents so they aren’t as

00:03:16.349 --> 00:03:17.890
easily blocked anymore.

00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:21.871
Opening it up, the hinge is now a lot easier
to open with one hand, but unfortunately,

00:03:21.871 --> 00:03:27.349
it gets pretty stiff before it reaches a comfortable
viewing angle, so you have to use your other

00:03:27.349 --> 00:03:28.349
hand anyway.

00:03:28.349 --> 00:03:29.349
Still happy about the improvement, though.

00:03:29.349 --> 00:03:32.739
On the inside, Dell chose to cover it with
their high quality carbon fiber composite

00:03:32.739 --> 00:03:36.019
finish, which protects from fingerprints,
and it actually feels really nice on your

00:03:36.019 --> 00:03:37.019
palm.

00:03:37.019 --> 00:03:40.959
Now looking at the display, you can see why
this laptop is so compact.

00:03:40.959 --> 00:03:46.930
Not only are the bezels incredibly thin all-around,
but the display is using a new 16:10 aspect

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:51.590
ratio, just like we love on our MacBooks,
which is great for adding more vertical space

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:52.939
for browsing the web.

00:03:52.939 --> 00:03:57.130
This was done by removing the Dell logo from
the bottom, which I think makes for a very

00:03:57.130 --> 00:04:00.900
clean look, and also relocating the webcam
above the display.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:05.409
With this move, they had to sacrifice Windows
Hello, but I think it was well worth it.

00:04:05.409 --> 00:04:10.670
Display quality itself is exceptional with
more than 4K resolution and great color accuracy

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:11.670
as well.

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:15.719
The keyboard is no longer recessed into the
casing as much as it was before, and it now

00:04:15.719 --> 00:04:19.970
basically covers the full-width of the laptop,
and it now looks incredibly clean.

00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:24.120
I love what they did with the fingerprint
sensor, switching from a circular power button

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:29.639
design on the standard XPS 13 to a stealthy-look
that imitates the keys surrounding it.

00:04:29.639 --> 00:04:33.729
The keys themselves are now wider with less
space between them, so they’re easier to

00:04:33.729 --> 00:04:35.250
accurately type on, and they’re now clickier
as well.

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:39.260
There seems to be a bit less key travel this
year, but it’s a really good middle-ground

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:42.550
compared to MacBook butterfly keys which noticeably
lack depth.

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:46.639
The trackpad actually works and feels pretty
nice, so we’re happy about that.

00:04:46.639 --> 00:04:51.130
Again, I’d like to mention the new cooling
system with a vapor chamber and hidden exhaust

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:52.940
fans to get it cool and quiet.

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:57.850
The down-firing speakers are louder and sound
pretty good with very clear vocals, but they’re

00:04:57.850 --> 00:04:59.170
still lacking volume.

00:04:59.170 --> 00:05:01.910
Just take the iPhone 11 Pro, for example.

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:12.620
While not being as balanced, they’re actually
louder than the speakers on this laptop.

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:27.660
***Sound comparison****

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:31.900
The XPS’s speakers definitely sound full
and really high quality, but we wish they

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:33.110
were a bit louder.

00:05:33.110 --> 00:05:39.180
A big plus is that you can get up to a 1TB
NVMe SSD, which now uses 4 lanes, so we’re

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:42.330
seeing improved storage transfer speeds as
you’ll see in a moment.

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:47.150
It’s also packing some killer wireless specs,
like Bluetooth 5.0 and top-of-the-line WiFi

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:50.660
6, which is basically as future-proof as you
can get right now.

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:55.180
As for ports, we still get a microSD card
slot, and now we get two Thunderbolt 3 ports

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:56.180
on this year’s 2-in-1, which is nice!

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:57.770
We just wish Dell would have thrown in one
or two extra USB-C ports on the more expensive

00:05:57.770 --> 00:05:58.770
models.

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:01.849
This year, all of the components are soldered
onto the motherboard, which is a downside

00:06:01.849 --> 00:06:05.690
for upgradability, but that’s the price
you have to pay to get such a thin and light,

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:06.690
premium design.

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:10.430
Now let’s get into performance benchmarks,
the most impressive thing about this laptop.

00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:15.790
For these, we’ll also toss in numbers from
the 2019 XPS 13 9380.

00:06:15.790 --> 00:06:20.229
In Geekbench 4’s CPU test, we’re seeing
an extremely high single-core score along

00:06:20.229 --> 00:06:24.419
with a very impressive multi-core score as
well, especially since we know it’s more

00:06:24.419 --> 00:06:25.669
efficient than before.

00:06:25.669 --> 00:06:30.039
In the graphics test, we also got a pretty
high OpenCL score, which isn’t as big of

00:06:30.039 --> 00:06:33.940
an improvement as we were expecting, but it’s
still really high considering it’s using

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:37.340
integrated graphics and not a dedicated graphics
chip.

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:43.750
And then in Cinebench R15, we got a score
of 781, which is improved over the XPS 13

00:06:43.750 --> 00:06:46.069
9380, but not that big of a deal.

00:06:46.069 --> 00:06:49.720
And finally, we tested storage speeds and
we’re seeing a good improvement over the

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:53.250
previous XPS, which will help with the overall
speed of the system.

00:06:53.250 --> 00:06:57.389
The best thing about the new 10th gen processor
is that we’re able to get even better performance

00:06:57.389 --> 00:07:02.050
in a thinner chassis while getting improved
battery life due to the increased efficiency.

00:07:02.050 --> 00:07:07.970
This is the fully-loaded model priced at $2700,
with a lot of the cost going to the 1TB NVMe

00:07:07.970 --> 00:07:12.539
SSD, but it’s awesome that you can get the
same exact 10th-gen Ice Lake i7 processor

00:07:12.539 --> 00:07:16.020
and Iris Plus graphics on the model that costs
$1650.

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:19.449
And that’s the one that we would recommend
for most people looking for a premium portable

00:07:19.449 --> 00:07:21.570
laptop with the best bang for the buck.

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:24.139
We’ll have links to these laptops down in
the description below.

00:07:24.139 --> 00:07:28.370
We also didn’t find any downsides with the
2-in-1 hinge, so if you don’t care at all

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:32.970
for using it as a tablet, you can still buy
this model and use it like a standard laptop.

00:07:32.970 --> 00:07:37.750
And to be completely honest, if you’re in
the market for a premium 13” Windows laptop,

00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:42.950
the 2019 XPS 13 2-in-1 is the best choice
out there, thanks to it being the first laptop

00:07:42.950 --> 00:07:48.880
packing the new 10nm Ice Lake processors and
great overall quality, delivering a very refined

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:49.949
laptop experience.

00:07:49.949 --> 00:07:54.099
If you enjoyed this review, make sure to tap
that like button and click the circle above

00:07:54.099 --> 00:07:58.020
to subscribe so you can see us compare it
to a similarly-priced 13” MacBook Pro.

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:03.710
Thanks for watching, and we’ll see you in
the next one!

